---
title: Buche de Noel (Chocolate Chestnut Log)
servings: 8
course: Dessert
locale: en
difficulty: Medium
---

= Base = 

Pre-heat the oven to 180°C. Grease a #20×30cm Swiss-roll tin{} and line with baking parchment so that it comes up 2.5 cm above the edge of the tin.

Separate the @eggs{6%large}, placing the whites in a large bowl and the yolks in a smaller bowl. Use an #electric hand whisk{} to whisk the egg yolks until they start to pale and thicken. Add the @caster sugar{150%g} and continue to whisk until the mixture becomes more thickened (but do not overdo this or it will go too stiff). Whisk in the @cocoa powder{50%g}(sifted) until it's thoroughly blended in. 

Wash the whisk heads in warm soapy water. When they are absolutely dry, clean and grease-free, whisk the egg whites until they form soft peaks. 

Spoon one lot of egg white into the chocolate mixture to loosen it. Carefully fold all of the chocolate mixture into the egg whites, until they are thoroughly combined. 

Spread the mixture into the prepared tin, giving it a few taps to even it out. Bake it in the centre of the oven for 15 to ~{20%minutes} or until it is risen and puffy and feels springy in the centre. Do not overcook. It is normal for it to sink quite a lot when removed from the oven.

Let the base cool completely. Place a sheet of baking parchment (about 2.5 cm larger all round than the Swiss-roll tin) on a work surface and sprinkle it with some @icing sugar{a little}. Loosen the edges of the chocolate base all round and turn it out on to the paper. Carefully peel off the base paper.

= Filling =

Empty the contents of the @sweetened chestnut purée{240%g} into a bowl, add 1 tbsp of the @double cream{275%ml}, and mix thoroughly. If the purée is unsweetened then also add some @caster sugar{}.

Using a small palette knife spread the mixture carefully and evenly all over the base. Sprinkle the @candied chestnuts{4}(chopped) all over.

Using the baking parchment roll the base over lengthways into a long roll, keeping it on the edge of the paper. Transfer it to a serving plate and then pull away the last of the paper.

= Christmas Snow Decoration =

Melt the @dark chocolate{50%g} in a bowl over some hot water. Beat the rest of the double cream (275 ml less 1 tbsp) until it is spreadable. Spread the cream evenly all over the log.

Drizzle a little trickle of melted chocolate up and down the length of the cream. Take a fork and, working only lengthways, blend the chocolate lightly into the cream, giving a woody bark-like effect. At the ends swirl the chocolate round.